<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yahya ibn Ibrahim was a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudala">Gudala</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> group of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, in the eleventh century. Returning from pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> around 1040, he met the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-imran-al-fasi">Abu Imran al-Fasi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, a contact that led to the scholar Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Yasin being sent to teach his people a stricter form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> treat this mission as the origin of the Almoravid movement.</p></div>
